  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Ensuring health workers are able to deliver quality, accurate, and non-discriminatory services is vital to building community trust and encouraging people to seek the services they need. In 2024, the Transformative Agenda Phase 2 program, implemented by UNFPA, provided training to 404 in-service health workers across nine countries to help them gain the skills to provide quality, inclusive family planning counselling, including learning how to deliver new methods of contraceptive protection. New long-acting contraceptive methods such as Jadelle, Implanon, and Sayana Press greatly increase women&apos;s and girls&apos; ability to choose a method that best suits them. In turn, this choice means they are more likely to continue using contraceptives and avoid unplanned pregnancies.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Under Phase 1 of the Transformative Agenda Kiribati, Samoa, Solomon Islands and Tonga revised their midwifery curriculum to align with international good standards. During this second phase Samoa commenced delivery of its updated pre-service midwifery curriculum aligned with international standards. Solomon Islands and Tonga prepared formal submissions of the revised curriculum for accreditation. Kiribati began recruitment of a qualitied midwifery teacher. Vanuatu completed a review of its existing midwifery curriculum which will be revised in 2024. These advancements will ensure that all midwives trained in these countries have training on rights based and inclusive service delivery that improves the experience of, and health outcomes for, clients, particularly women and girls.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
